### audio path evidence, zl1, 2026-09-22
### Question: user asked for "音频：让扬声器真的出声". What can be proven without an ear?

===== 1. the software stack is already wired, and to the right port =====
$ pactl list short sinks            (as phablet, XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/32011)
0  sink.fake.sco        module-null-sink.c    s16le 1ch 8000Hz   SUSPENDED
1  sink.primary_output  module-droid-card.c    s16le 2ch 44100Hz  SUSPENDED     <- default sink
   Active Port: output-speaker        Mute: no      Volume: 65536 / 100% / 0.00 dB

$ pactl list short modules | grep droid
6  module-droid-discover   voice_virtual_stream=true hidl_args='helper=false'
7  module-droid-card-28    voice_virtual_stream=true quirks=-output_deep_buffer
8  module-droid-hidl       helper=false

So: one droid card, one output, its active port is already the speaker, unmuted, at full volume,
and the HIDL bridge module is loaded. (helper=false -- this port does not use the
audiosystem-passthrough helper process for the output path.)

===== 2. pulseaudio feeds it, and the Android HAL routes it to the speaker =====
$ paplay --device=sink.primary_output /tmp/zl1-audio-test.wav     (12 s, 440 Hz)
   during playback:  sink.primary_output ... RUNNING
   after:            sink.primary_output ... SUSPENDED      (module-suspend-on-idle, normal)

container logcat, audio_hw_primary, one line per playback:
  start_output_stream: enter: stream(0x5f2b556090)usecase(1: low-latency-playback) devices(0x2)
  select_devices: out_snd_device(2: speaker-stereo) in_snd_device(0: )
  enable_snd_device: snd_device(2: speaker-stereo)
  enable_audio_route: apply mixer and update path: low-latency-playback smartpa
  start_output_stream: exit
  ... then at standby:
  out_standby: enter: stream ... usecase(1: low-latency-playback)
  disable_audio_route: reset and update mixer path: low-latency-playback smartpa
  disable_snd_device: snd_device(2: speaker-stereo)
devices(0x2) = AUDIO_DEVICE_OUT_SPEAKER. No error, no fallback, no "(none)" device.

===== 3. the codec really is configured for the speaker while it plays, and torn down after =====
read with /system/bin/tinymix (see §4 for why not amixer)

  control                                      during playback      at idle
  TERT_MI2S_RX Audio Mixer MultiMedia5         On                   Off
  Speaker Volume                               5                    1
  Digital Gain                                 56                   40
  Boost Output Voltage                         9V                   6.5V
  Left Channel Enable                          On                   Off
  Right Channel Enable                         On                   Off
  Left Feedback Enable                         ON                   ON
  Right Feedback Enable                        ON                   ON
  AIF4_VI Mixer SPKR_VI_1 / _2                 Off                  Off

  -> the backend is TERT_MI2S_RX, i.e. **the tertiary MI2S**, not SLIMBUS_0_RX (the internal
     WCD9335 codec). On the `speaker` path of /vendor/etc/mixer_paths_tasha.xml the same controls
     are set to exactly these playback values (Boost 9V, Volume 5, Digital Gain 56, both channel
     enables 1), so the HAL is applying that path and then un-applying it at standby -- the
     difference between the two columns IS the evidence that playback configured the amp.
  -> SPKR_VI_1/2 (the amplifier's voltage/current sense input to the codec, i.e. speaker
     protection) stays Off in both columns. Whether it is required for output on this board is
     not established here.

===== 4. the host PCM that carries it =====
while the tone played, exactly one host PCM was RUNNING:
  /proc/asound/card0/pcm15p/sub0/status: state: RUNNING
device 15 = MultiMedia5, which is the PCM the TERT_MI2S_RX route above feeds. The digital path
therefore reaches the SoC's MI2S output port and the kernel's DMA, not just the HAL.

===== 5. the amplifier is a known backend, and its calibration loads =====
logcat at boot:
  msm8974_platform: platform_set_snd_device_backend: backend_tag_table[speaker]: old = null new = smartpa
  msm8974_platform: platform_set_snd_device_backend: backend_tag_table[voice-speaker]: old = null new = smartpa
  ACDB-LOADER: ACDB -> Load file: /vendor/etc/acdbdata/MTP/MTP_Speaker_cal.acdb
  ThermalEngine: Found section 'SPEAKER-CAL' / Parsing section SPEAKER-CAL
So the platform driver maps the speaker to a "smartpa" backend and the MTP speaker calibration
file is loaded without error.

===== 6. a tool trap worth recording: the host's amixer cannot read this card =====
$ amixer -c 0 scontrols
amixer: Mixer sysdefault:0 load error: No such device
$ amixer -D hw:0 scontrols
amixer: Mixer hw:0 load error: No such device
$ amixer -c 0 info
Card sysdefault:0 'msm8996tashamtp'/'msm8996-tasha-mtp-snd-card'   Mixer name : ''
but:
$ /system/bin/tinymix            # straight from the host, no nsenter
Mixer name: 'msm8996-tasha-mtp-snd-card'
Number of controls: 2392
So this is a *tool* difference, not a namespace one: Halium symlinks /system -> /android/system,
the Android tinymix runs as-is, and no PID-namespace change is needed to read the codec (unlike
the sensors HAL, which does need one). Anyone reaching for amixer here will wrongly conclude the
codec is absent.

===== 7. what is NOT proven =====
Whether any sound is actually audible. Nothing above can measure that; the phone's speaker is the
only instrument, and it belongs to the user. Note also that the first attempt used a 440 Hz tone
at amplitude 12000/32767 -- a small phone speaker is very inefficient at 440 Hz, so "quiet or
silent" at 440 Hz is not evidence of a fault. The audible test used 1.8 kHz / 2.4 kHz beeps at
amplitude 30000.

If it is audible, the software chain is complete end to end and nothing here needs changing.
If it is silent, the remaining suspects are all downstream of the codec and all outside this
software path: the external smart amplifier's power enable, its I2C probe/configuration, or its
firmware -- which needs the boot-time kernel log, and this device's kernel log is **not** kept
(`journalctl -k -b` -> "No entries"; the ring buffer wraps in about a minute), so that would have
to be captured during a boot.
